    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15993: A 72-year-old male presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #54583: A 19-year-old female presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Psoriasis vulgaris", "Tinea corporis", "Lichen planus", "Nummular eczema", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72093: A 78-year-old girl presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25926: A 35-year-old male with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Blue nevus", "Dermatofibroma", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20423: A 20-year-old boy presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10158: A 12-year-old man with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Mohs micrographic surgery", "Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Oral vismodegib therapy", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #29979: A 78-year-old man presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Pembrolizumab", "High-dose systemic Prednisone", "Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45613: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61236: A 62-year-old man with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Plaque psoriasis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38310: A dermatologist evaluates a 38-year-old man with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Field cancerization", "Metastatic suppression", "Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Keratolytic occlusion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32882: A 50-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Keratoacanthoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69909: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Bowenoid papulosis", "Pearly penile papules", "Condyloma acuminata"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #29473: A 50-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision", "The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18414: A 25-year-old girl organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "The risk is identical to the general population", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84457: A 35-year-old girl presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks", "Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85507: A 58-year-old girl presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Marjolin''s ulcer", "Venous stasis ulcer", "Cushing''s ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Curling''s ulcer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82716: A 45-year-old girl presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #44442: A 25-year-old female presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13213: An 82-year-old male presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Melasma", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Seborrheic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81068: A 65-year-old African American girl presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #54565: A 62-year-old male presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis", "Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16234: A 68-year-old woman presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Pyogenic granuloma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Spitz nevus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79694: A 12-year-old boy undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["Clark''s level of anatomic invasion", "The presence of regression", "The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25868: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80332: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 68-year-old girl reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins", "A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #36427: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas", "It prevents the development of systemic hypertension"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #83040: A 28-year-old male with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64894: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59963: A 25-year-old male presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94646: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 25-year-old boy. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #96109: An 80-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Small cell lung cancer metastasis", "Merkel Cell Carcinoma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64244: A 68-year-old female is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #77132: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Bartonella henselae", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Treponema pallidum", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21835: A 35-year-old boy with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)", "Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy", "Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Interferon-alpha injections"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88044: A 55-year-old girl presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen simplex chronicus", "Lichen planus", "Asteatotic eczema", "Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #54061: A 19-year-old male with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch", "Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60092: A 70-year-old female presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Atopic dermatitis", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Sezary Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39099: A 35-year-old female presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibroma", "Kaposi Sarcoma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Keloid scar"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #48638: A 19-year-old girl with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Pembrolizumab", "Vemurafenib", "Vismodegib", "Rituximab", "Imatinib mesylate"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86308: A 35-year-old boy presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["A diameter greater than 6 mm", "An asymmetric shape", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24697: A 35-year-old male presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #48159: A 17-year-old woman presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Psoriasis vulgaris", "Nummular eczema", "Tinea corporis", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Lichen planus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72163: A 35-year-old girl presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32730: A 72-year-old boy with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Blue nevus", "Seborrheic keratosis", "Dermatofibroma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #65165: A 20-year-old girl presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38301: A 17-year-old man with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Oral vismodegib therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88314: A 78-year-old girl presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Pembrolizumab", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib", "High-dose systemic Prednisone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88568: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59364: A 78-year-old girl with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic Keratosis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50406: A dermatologist evaluates a 55-year-old female with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Keratolytic occlusion", "Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Field cancerization", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Metastatic suppression"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10418: A 38-year-old boy presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Keratoacanthoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47437: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Condyloma acuminata", "Bowenoid papulosis", "Pearly penile papules", "Balanitis xerotica obliterans"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68796: A 20-year-old boy presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #48424: A 68-year-old man organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "The risk is identical to the general population", "A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #65063: A 50-year-old man presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35859: A 58-year-old boy presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Venous stasis ulcer", "Curling''s ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Cushing''s ulcer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10549: A 20-year-old boy presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Amelanotic Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #51468: A 78-year-old boy presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62703: An 82-year-old woman presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Melasma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59754: A 65-year-old African American woman presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Mucosal Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13715: A 78-year-old man presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis", "Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93949: A 35-year-old man presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Spitz nevus", "Pyogenic granuloma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59652: A 55-year-old man undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)", "The presence of regression", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93068: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57591: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 17-year-old female reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway"]'::jsonb, 4, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80910: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47928: A 20-year-old man with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63761: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37795: A 25-year-old woman presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59422: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 28-year-old man. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16995: An 80-year-old boy presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Merkel Cell Carcinoma", "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43243: A 19-year-old female is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #73268: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Bartonella henselae", "Treponema pallidum", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #19677: A 12-year-old man with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy", "Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)", "Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #29729: A 55-year-old boy presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Lichen planus", "Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #19827: A 28-year-old woman with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch", "Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81991: A 70-year-old man presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sezary Syndrome", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Atopic dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93220: A 35-year-old female presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibroma", "Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Kaposi Sarcoma", "Keloid scar", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56125: A 38-year-old girl with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Imatinib mesylate", "Rituximab", "Pembrolizumab", "Vismodegib", "Vemurafenib"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28593: A 78-year-old male presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)", "A diameter greater than 6 mm", "An asymmetric shape", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23405: A 72-year-old girl presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75732: A 78-year-old boy presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Tinea corporis", "Psoriasis vulgaris", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Lichen planus", "Nummular eczema"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56741: A 78-year-old boy presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)", "It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82111: A 12-year-old male with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Blue nevus", "Dermatofibroma", "Seborrheic keratosis", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11489: A 20-year-old woman presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88596: A 78-year-old male with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)", "Oral vismodegib therapy", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84982: A 78-year-old female presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib", "Pembrolizumab", "Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "High-dose systemic Prednisone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50082: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia", "Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #73863: A 72-year-old girl with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Plaque psoriasis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56040: A dermatologist evaluates a 28-year-old girl with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Keratolytic occlusion", "Field cancerization", "Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Metastatic suppression"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75257: A 12-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Keratoacanthoma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Actinic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70270: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Condyloma acuminata", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Bowenoid papulosis", "Pearly penile papules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64075: A 78-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13797: A 28-year-old female organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["The risk is identical to the general population", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #65534: A 78-year-old female presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61536: A 58-year-old male presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Curling''s ulcer", "Cushing''s ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Venous stasis ulcer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78240: A 28-year-old female presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #58173: A 38-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91651: An 82-year-old woman presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Actinic Keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Melasma", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #96190: A 65-year-old African American male presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94127: A 50-year-old man presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15157: A 62-year-old female presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Pyogenic granuloma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Spitz nevus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #67279: A 35-year-old boy undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["The presence of regression", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62586: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin", "CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56981: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 45-year-old male reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway"]'::jsonb, 4, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91492: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It prevents the development of systemic hypertension"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57015: A 20-year-old girl with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #77813: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91358: A 25-year-old woman presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #55371: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 72-year-old male. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85251: An 80-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis", "Merkel Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82851: A 62-year-old boy is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88023: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Bartonella henselae", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Treponema pallidum", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #55473: A 45-year-old boy with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35726: A 55-year-old girl presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #19046: A 28-year-old girl with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37737: A 70-year-old boy presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Atopic dermatitis", "Sezary Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #77965: A 35-year-old girl presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibroma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Keloid scar", "Kaposi Sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78114: A 12-year-old man with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Vismodegib", "Pembrolizumab", "Vemurafenib", "Imatinib mesylate", "Rituximab"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61004: A 38-year-old man presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["A diameter greater than 6 mm", "An asymmetric shape", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64941: A 38-year-old female presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81269: A 55-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Lichen planus", "Nummular eczema", "Tinea corporis", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Psoriasis vulgaris"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89329: A 78-year-old girl presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)", "It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #12771: A 78-year-old boy with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Blue nevus", "Dermatofibroma", "Seborrheic keratosis", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57559: A 20-year-old man presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92171: A 45-year-old female with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Oral vismodegib therapy", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88736: A 78-year-old man presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib", "High-dose systemic Prednisone", "Pembrolizumab", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #29957: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62613: A 50-year-old male with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic Keratosis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Plaque psoriasis", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus", "Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32071: A dermatologist evaluates a 19-year-old woman with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Keratolytic occlusion", "Field cancerization", "Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Metastatic suppression", "Autoimmune desensitization"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75967: A 17-year-old female presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Keratoacanthoma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87351: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Bowenoid papulosis", "Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Pearly penile papules", "Condyloma acuminata"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64979: A 12-year-old man presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68820: A 25-year-old male organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["The risk is identical to the general population", "A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #51144: A 78-year-old female presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks", "Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #51101: A 58-year-old man presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Marjolin''s ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Cushing''s ulcer", "Venous stasis ulcer", "Curling''s ulcer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53648: A 55-year-old male presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Amelanotic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91428: A 20-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60349: An 82-year-old woman presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic Keratosis", "Melasma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66628: A 65-year-old African American male presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72674: A 20-year-old female presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60437: A 12-year-old woman presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Pyogenic granuloma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Spitz nevus", "Amelanotic Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35429: A 68-year-old boy undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "The presence of regression", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13450: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30679: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 12-year-old male reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31701: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78444: A 45-year-old woman with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75926: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76146: A 25-year-old boy presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89731: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 12-year-old boy. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy", "Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70724: An 80-year-old man presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Merkel Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43483: A 45-year-old male is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80434: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Treponema pallidum", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Bartonella henselae", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66085: A 17-year-old male with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy", "Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72635: A 55-year-old man presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen planus", "Asteatotic eczema", "Plaque psoriasis", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70422: A 25-year-old girl with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #33696: A 70-year-old female presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)", "Atopic dermatitis", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Sezary Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89948: A 35-year-old man presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Kaposi Sarcoma", "Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Keloid scar", "Dermatofibroma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90591: A 62-year-old girl with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Vemurafenib", "Rituximab", "Vismodegib", "Imatinib mesylate", "Pembrolizumab"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30289: A 68-year-old woman presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["A diameter greater than 6 mm", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)", "An asymmetric shape", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20643: A 45-year-old girl presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66485: A 25-year-old man presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Psoriasis vulgaris", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Lichen planus", "Nummular eczema", "Tinea corporis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39878: A 38-year-old girl presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46991: A 68-year-old male with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibroma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Seborrheic keratosis", "Blue nevus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72158: A 20-year-old man presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30053: A 28-year-old man with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)", "Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Oral vismodegib therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50102: A 78-year-old girl presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Pembrolizumab", "High-dose systemic Prednisone", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66659: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia", "Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75652: A 62-year-old male with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Plaque psoriasis", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus", "Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #71968: A dermatologist evaluates a 17-year-old female with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Keratolytic occlusion", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Field cancerization", "Metastatic suppression"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94741: A 50-year-old girl presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Keratoacanthoma", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37374: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Pearly penile papules", "Condyloma acuminata", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Bowenoid papulosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75153: A 25-year-old boy presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62846: A 38-year-old boy organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "The risk is identical to the general population", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76059: A 62-year-old female presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25985: A 58-year-old girl presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Cushing''s ulcer", "Venous stasis ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Curling''s ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22323: A 12-year-old woman presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95903: A 68-year-old man presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Nodular Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66411: An 82-year-old girl presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)", "Melasma", "Seborrheic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18864: A 65-year-old African American woman presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Nodular Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92201: A 35-year-old boy presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63056: A 78-year-old woman presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Pyogenic granuloma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Spitz nevus", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #36975: A 62-year-old boy undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "The presence of regression", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46053: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82801: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 19-year-old female reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins", "A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway"]'::jsonb, 4, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60540: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34418: A 55-year-old woman with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24463: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28987: A 25-year-old woman presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40244: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 72-year-old man. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread", "Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43760: An 80-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Merkel Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25480: A 12-year-old male is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81866: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Treponema pallidum", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Bartonella henselae"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24590: A 12-year-old female with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #55407: A 55-year-old boy presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen planus", "Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)", "Asteatotic eczema", "Plaque psoriasis", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13940: A 17-year-old man with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch", "Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93349: A 70-year-old male presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sezary Syndrome", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Atopic dermatitis", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #17463: A 35-year-old female presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Kaposi Sarcoma", "Dermatofibroma", "Keloid scar"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13689: A 12-year-old woman with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Vemurafenib", "Rituximab", "Imatinib mesylate", "Pembrolizumab", "Vismodegib"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31252: A 17-year-old boy presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)", "An asymmetric shape", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "A diameter greater than 6 mm", "Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81421: A 38-year-old woman presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis", "Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #44766: A 62-year-old boy presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Tinea corporis", "Nummular eczema", "Lichen planus", "Psoriasis vulgaris", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80085: A 55-year-old female presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins", "It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75622: A 50-year-old female with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibroma", "Seborrheic keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Blue nevus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34342: A 20-year-old man presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63124: A 78-year-old male with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Oral vismodegib therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70945: A 78-year-old man presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Pembrolizumab", "High-dose systemic Prednisone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #42169: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia", "Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61571: A 28-year-old girl with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94237: A dermatologist evaluates a 12-year-old female with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Keratolytic occlusion", "Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Metastatic suppression", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Field cancerization"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16691: A 19-year-old man presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Keratoacanthoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25783: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Condyloma acuminata", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Pearly penile papules", "Bowenoid papulosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #19912: A 20-year-old man presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53672: A 20-year-old female organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation", "The risk is identical to the general population", "A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50836: A 55-year-old woman presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks", "Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85116: A 58-year-old man presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Venous stasis ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer", "Curling''s ulcer", "Cushing''s ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23064: A 17-year-old girl presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81371: A 78-year-old female presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72922: An 82-year-old girl presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Melasma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57281: A 65-year-old African American man presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94285: A 45-year-old female presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39659: A 68-year-old female presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Spitz nevus", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Pyogenic granuloma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98531: A 25-year-old girl undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)", "The presence of regression", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21672: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin", "CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79357: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 78-year-old girl reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins"]'::jsonb, 1, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #65907: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70761: A 45-year-old man with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76534: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21697: A 25-year-old woman presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69400: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 45-year-old boy. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #77048: An 80-year-old female presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma", "Merkel Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59024: A 35-year-old girl is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94248: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Treponema pallidum", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Bartonella henselae"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #52754: A 12-year-old woman with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)", "Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23882: A 55-year-old girl presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen simplex chronicus", "Asteatotic eczema", "Plaque psoriasis", "Lichen planus", "Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64574: A 28-year-old female with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30176: A 70-year-old woman presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sezary Syndrome", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Atopic dermatitis", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46605: A 35-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Keloid scar", "Dermatofibroma", "Kaposi Sarcoma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81286: A 38-year-old female with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Vemurafenib", "Pembrolizumab", "Imatinib mesylate", "Rituximab", "Vismodegib"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10230: A 35-year-old boy presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["An asymmetric shape", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)", "Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)", "A diameter greater than 6 mm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11771: A 19-year-old man presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41606: A 45-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Tinea corporis", "Nummular eczema", "Psoriasis vulgaris", "Lichen planus", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27480: A 78-year-old man presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87640: A 19-year-old girl with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Blue nevus", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Dermatofibroma", "Seborrheic keratosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28532: A 20-year-old male presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98837: A 28-year-old female with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Oral vismodegib therapy", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89072: A 78-year-old woman presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "High-dose systemic Prednisone", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib", "Pembrolizumab"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20925: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #51680: A 17-year-old male with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis", "Plaque psoriasis", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76670: A dermatologist evaluates a 45-year-old male with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Field cancerization", "Keratolytic occlusion", "Metastatic suppression"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #36228: A 20-year-old girl presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Actinic Keratosis", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Keratoacanthoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86316: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Bowenoid papulosis", "Pearly penile papules", "Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Condyloma acuminata"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21985: A 35-year-old girl presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10690: A 45-year-old female organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "The risk is identical to the general population", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98566: A 62-year-old man presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18718: A 58-year-old boy presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Venous stasis ulcer", "Curling''s ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer", "Cushing''s ulcer"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #71439: A 78-year-old boy presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #26450: A 25-year-old boy presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41019: An 82-year-old boy presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Actinic Keratosis", "Melasma", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86195: A 65-year-old African American woman presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Mucosal Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61810: A 17-year-old male presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis", "Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82487: A 35-year-old girl presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Amelanotic Melanoma", "Spitz nevus", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Pyogenic granuloma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45969: A 19-year-old female undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["The presence of regression", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)", "The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94578: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45974: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 12-year-old girl reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway", "A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper"]'::jsonb, 0, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24940: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18243: A 28-year-old male with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32619: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37068: A 25-year-old female presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90158: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 35-year-old woman. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50388: An 80-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Merkel Cell Carcinoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57870: A 25-year-old woman is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86913: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Treponema pallidum", "Bartonella henselae", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72142: A 12-year-old boy with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25134: A 55-year-old male presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)", "Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39791: A 78-year-old girl with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #52026: A 70-year-old boy presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Atopic dermatitis", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Sezary Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47341: A 35-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Keloid scar", "Kaposi Sarcoma", "Dermatofibroma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #29244: A 55-year-old male with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Rituximab", "Vismodegib", "Imatinib mesylate", "Pembrolizumab", "Vemurafenib"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92560: A 35-year-old girl presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)", "A diameter greater than 6 mm", "An asymmetric shape"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87485: A 19-year-old girl presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79422: A 38-year-old man presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Nummular eczema", "Psoriasis vulgaris", "Lichen planus", "Tinea corporis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60902: A 28-year-old boy presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins", "It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24603: A 50-year-old female with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Blue nevus", "Dermatofibroma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Seborrheic keratosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91067: A 20-year-old girl presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74916: A 35-year-old woman with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Photodynamic therapy (PDT)", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Oral vismodegib therapy", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23055: A 78-year-old male presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["Pembrolizumab", "High-dose systemic Prednisone", "Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80082: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10612: A 38-year-old man with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Plaque psoriasis", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus", "Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #12715: A dermatologist evaluates a 17-year-old girl with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Metastatic suppression", "Autoimmune desensitization", "Field cancerization", "Keratolytic occlusion", "Contact hypersensitivity induction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16761: A 20-year-old woman presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Keratoacanthoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43973: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Condyloma acuminata", "Bowenoid papulosis", "Pearly penile papules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #19021: A 20-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79544: A 25-year-old boy organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation", "A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "The risk is identical to the general population"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47074: A 55-year-old woman presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks", "Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92160: A 58-year-old male presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Cushing''s ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer", "Curling''s ulcer", "Venous stasis ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47233: A 17-year-old woman presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #49104: A 17-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57402: An 82-year-old girl presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Actinic Keratosis", "Melasma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72901: A 65-year-old African American man presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39682: A 50-year-old male presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis", "Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57956: A 35-year-old girl presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Spitz nevus", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Pyogenic granuloma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18625: A 55-year-old woman undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "Clark''s level of anatomic invasion", "The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "The presence of regression", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69497: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #71687: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 38-year-old male reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway", "A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper"]'::jsonb, 2, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76770: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #44919: A 78-year-old man with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95168: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #65633: A 25-year-old woman presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81145: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 28-year-old girl. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy", "Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61335: An 80-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-to-purple nodule on their sun-damaged forehead. The lesion appeared only 3 weeks ago and has grown to 1.5 cm. Biopsy shows a dense dermal infiltrate of small, round, blue cells with sparse cytoplasm and high mitotic activity. Immunohistochemical stains are positive for Cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in a characteristic ''perinuclear dot'' pattern. What is the diagnosis?', '["Amelanotic Melanoma", "Merkel Cell Carcinoma", "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma", "Small cell lung cancer metastasis", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Merkel Cell Carcinoma is a highly aggressive primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin that classically presents as a rapidly growing, painless, red-purple nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly patients. Histologically, it is a ''small round blue cell'' tumor. The pathognomonic diagnostic finding on IHC is CK20 positivity showing a distinctive ''perinuclear dot'' or ''cap'' pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84400: A 38-year-old girl is diagnosed with Merkel Cell Carcinoma. What virus is integrated into the host genome in approximately 80% of these highly aggressive tumors?', '["Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Merkel Cell Polyomavirus (MCPyV), a double-stranded DNA virus, is clonal-integrated into the host cell genome in approximately 80% of Merkel Cell Carcinomas, expressing oncogenic T-antigens that drive cell cycle progression and inhibit tumor suppressors like p53 and Rb.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20688: A 32-year-old HIV-positive male with a CD4 count of 120/mm3 presents with multiple violaceous, non-blanching macules and nodules on his lower extremities, trunk, and hard palate. Biopsy reveals a vascular proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like spaces containing extravasated red blood cells. What is the causative organism of this disease?', '["Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)", "Treponema pallidum", "Bartonella henselae", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Kaposi Sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor driven by Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also known as Kaposi Sarcoma-associated Herpesvirus (KSHV). It classically presents as red, purple, or brown non-blanching violaceous patches, plaques, or nodules, frequently involving the skin, oral mucosa (hard palate), and GI tract in immunocompromised (AIDS) individuals. HIV acts synergistically but HHV-8 is the necessary causative pathogen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61466: A 55-year-old male with newly diagnosed AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma is evaluated. Aside from localized cutaneous lesions, they have no visceral involvement or systemic symptoms. What is the first-line therapy to initiate for this patient?', '["Local radiation therapy to the bilateral lower extremities", "Wide local surgical excision of all skin spots", "Intravenous liposomal doxorubicin chemotherapy", "Interferon-alpha injections", "Initiate Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For mild-to-moderate, slowly progressive, or localized AIDS-related Kaposi Sarcoma, the first-line therapeutic intervention is the initiation or optimization of Highly Active Antiretroviral Therapy (HAART). Restoring the CD4 count and suppressing HIV viral replication allows the patient''s immune system to clear HHV-8 infected cells, frequently leading to complete resolution of the KS lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85455: A 55-year-old male presents with a 5-year history of a highly pruritic rash on their buttocks and inner thighs that has been misdiagnosed as ''eczema'' and treated with multiple steroid creams. Physical exam shows several large, thin, dusky-red patches with a finely wrinkled, ''cigarette-paper'' surface. Biopsy reveals a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis with prominent epidermotropism and Pautrier microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis Fungoides (Patch stage)", "Plaque psoriasis", "Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycosis Fungoides (MF) is the most common form of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). In its early patch stage, it classically presents as thin, erythematous, slightly scaly, and pruritic patches in a non-sun-exposed ''bathing suit'' distribution (buttocks, groin, thighs). Key pathologic findings include epidermotropism (migration of malignant T-cells into the epidermis without spongiosis) and Pautrier microabscesses (small epidermal clusters of atypical T-cells).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45533: A 25-year-old male with a history of patch-stage Mycosis Fungoides presents for follow-up. They have developed multiple thick, indurated, elevated red-brown plaques on their trunk. What is the classic progression sequence of cutaneous lesions in this lymphoma?', '["Erythroderma -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Tumor -> Plaque", "Plaque -> Patch -> Tumor", "Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor", "Tumor -> Plaque -> Patch"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mycosis Fungoides classically follows a slow, progressive clinical course over years or decades, transitioning sequentially through three distinct cutaneous stages: Patch stage (flat, scaly macules/patches), Plaque stage (thickened, elevated, indurated plaques), and finally Tumor stage (large, dome-shaped, frequently ulcerating nodules/tumors with high risk of systemic dissemination).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35319: A 70-year-old boy presents with severe, generalized skin itching, hair loss, and bright red skin covering 95% of their body surface area. They also have marked palmoplantar thickening and enlarged lymph nodes. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical T-lymphocytes with highly folded, ''cerebriform'' nuclei. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sezary Syndrome", "Erythrodermic psoriasis", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)", "Mycosis Fungoides (Tumor stage)", "Atopic dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sezary Syndrome is the leukemic (systemic) variant of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of: 1) Generalized erythroderma (erythema covering >90% BSA), 2) Generalized lymphadenopathy, and 3) Circulating malignant T-lymphocytes (Sezary cells) featuring a highly folded, brain-like, ''cerebriform'' nucleus, along with a helper T-cell predominance (CD4:CD8 ratio >10).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15502: A 35-year-old female presents with a slow-growing, very firm, flesh-colored to violaceous plaque on their abdominal wall that has been present for 2 years. Biopsy shows a highly cellular spindle cell proliferation arranged in a ''storiform'' (mat-like or wheel-spoke) pattern, invading the subcutaneous fat in a characteristic ''honeycomb'' fashion. The cells stain positive for CD34. What is the diagnosis?', '["Kaposi Sarcoma", "Dermatofibroma", "Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP)", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Keloid scar"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is a slow-growing, locally aggressive spindle-cell sarcoma of the dermis, most commonly arising on the trunk or proximal extremities of young adults. Classic histopathology shows a monomorphic proliferation of spindle cells arranged in a storiform (mat-like) pattern that infiltrates the subcutaneous fat, producing a characteristic ''honeycomb'' appearance. DFSP cells are strongly CD34-positive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89115: A 72-year-old male with a confirmed Dermatofibrosarcoma Protuberans (DFSP) is found to have a pathognomonic t(17;22) translocation, resulting in the fusion of the COL1A1 and PDGFB genes. For unresectable or metastatic DFSP, what targeted medication specifically inhibits the receptor tyrosine kinase activated by this fusion protein?', '["Imatinib mesylate", "Pembrolizumab", "Vismodegib", "Vemurafenib", "Rituximab"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The t(17;22)(q22;q13) translocation in DFSP fuses the collagen type I alpha 1 (COL1A1) gene with the platelet-derived growth factor beta (PDGFB) gene, leading to continuous, autocrine overproduction of PDGFB ligand. This activates the PDGF receptor tyrosine kinase, driving cell growth. The tyrosine kinase inhibitor Imatinib mesylate specifically blocks this activation and is FDA-approved for unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic DFSP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18058: A 17-year-old boy presents with multiple flat-topped, violaceous, itchy papules on their wrists. A skin biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes that while the clinical differential included lichen planus and early skin cancer, the histology is completely benign, showing hyperkeratosis, a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction, and saw-tooth rete ridges. Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic clinical warning sign of cutaneous melanoma?', '["A diameter greater than 6 mm", "An asymmetric shape", "Recent rapid growth or changes in the lesion (evolving)", "A symmetric, uniform brown color with a perfectly smooth border", "Variegation of color (different shades of brown, black, red, or blue)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A symmetric, uniform brown color with a smooth, round border is characteristic of a benign acquired melanocytic nevus. In contrast, the classic warning signs of cutaneous melanoma are represented by the ABCDE criteria: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, and Evolving (recent changes in size, shape, color, or symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15749: A 55-year-old boy presents with a non-healing bump on their nose that has been present for 8 months and bleeds occasionally after drying their face. Physical exam shows a 6mm shiny, flesh-colored papule with central ulceration and prominent arborizing telangiectasias under dermoscopy. What is the classic histologic feature of this lesion?', '["Islands of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction artifact", "Perinuclear dot-like positivity of cytokeratin 20 (CK20) in small round blue cells", "Proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces containing red blood cells", "Concentric nests of keratinizing squamous cells forming keratin pearls in the dermis", "Atypical melanocytes arranged in pagetoid spread throughout the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical description of a pearly papule with telangiectasias and a central ''rodent ulcer'' is classic for nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Histopathology classically demonstrates islands or nests of atypical basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and retraction (shrinkage) artifact from the surrounding specialized stroma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64820: A 55-year-old man presents with a slow-growing, red, scaly, and slightly shiny plaque on their upper back. They have been applying over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream for 6 months without any improvement. A punch biopsy is performed. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Tinea corporis", "Psoriasis vulgaris", "Nummular eczema", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Lichen planus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma is a common subtype of BCC that classically presents as a thin, erythematous, scaly, well-demarcated plaque, most commonly on the trunk or extremities. It is frequently misdiagnosed and treated as an inflammatory dermatosis (like eczema or psoriasis) due to its flat, scaly appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27879: A 78-year-old man presents with a firm, white, scar-like plaque on their temple, although they deny any prior trauma or surgery to the area. Physical exam reveals an ill-defined, indurated, ivory-white plaque with indistinct borders. What is the clinical significance of this specific subtype of Basal Cell Carcinoma?', '["It is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, which has high clinical aggressiveness and significant subclinical spread, necessitating Mohs micrographic surgery", "It is associated with an underlying visceral malignancy (Muir-Torre syndrome)", "It is highly responsive to topical 5-fluorouracil field therapy and does not require surgery", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression and can be safely observed", "It is highly metastatic to the regional lymph nodes and requires wide local excision with 2 cm margins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Morpheaform (sclerosing) BCC classically presents as a firm, scar-like, ivory-white or flesh-colored indurated plaque. It is highly aggressive and has extensive subclinical (microscopic) extension far beyond its visible borders. Because of these ill-defined borders and high risk of recurrence, Mohs micrographic surgery is the gold standard treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20131: A 20-year-old man with a Fitzpatrick skin phototype IV presents with a dark brown-to-black, slightly shiny, 5mm papule on their cheek. Dermoscopy reveals large blue-gray ovoid nests, spoke-wheel areas, and leaf-like structures, with an absence of a pigment network. What is the diagnosis?', '["Blue nevus", "Dermatofibroma", "Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Seborrheic keratosis", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma contains melanin produced by benign melanocytes colonizing the tumor. It is the most common presentation of BCC in darker-skinned individuals (Fitzpatrick phototypes IV-VI). Classic dermoscopic findings include blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like structures, and spoke-wheel areas, without the atypical pigment network characteristic of melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13940: A 20-year-old male presents with multiple basal cell carcinomas on their face and trunk. Physical examination reveals palmoplantar pits, frontal bossing, and hypertelorism. The patient also has a history of an odontogenic keratocyst removed from the jaw. What is the primary molecular defect driving this patient''s syndrome?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the CDKN2A gene, disrupting cell cycle regulation", "Defective nucleotide excision repair (NER) of UV-induced thymine dimers", "Gain-of-function mutation in the BRAF gene (V600E), causing overactive MAP kinase signaling", "Inactivation of the p53 tumor suppressor gene via HPV E6 protein", "Loss-of-function mutation in the PTCH1 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation in the PTCH1 (Patched) tumor suppressor gene. PTCH1 normally inhibits Smoothened (SMO). Loss of PTCH1 leads to constitutive activation of the Hedgehog pathway, resulting in multiple early-onset BCCs, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and calcification of the falx cerebri.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #52118: A 45-year-old male with Gorlin syndrome is diagnosed with dozens of new Basal Cell Carcinomas. Which of the following therapeutic modalities should be AVOIDED in this patient due to the risk of triggering an explosive eruption of new skin cancers?', '["Oral vismodegib therapy", "Topical imiquimod 5% cream", "Mohs micrographic surgery", "Ionizing radiation (Radiation therapy)", "Photodynamic therapy (PDT)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In patients with Gorlin syndrome (Nevoid BCC syndrome), exposure to ionizing radiation (radiation therapy) triggers genomic instability that leads to the rapid, explosive eruption of hundreds of new BCCs within the radiation field. Thus, radiation therapy is strictly contraindicated or avoided in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23256: A 78-year-old girl presents with a massive, locally advanced, and highly invasive Basal Cell Carcinoma of the scalp that has eroded through the skull into the dura mater. The neurosurgical and dermatologic teams determine the tumor is completely unresectable. What is the first-line systemic therapy for this advanced tumor?', '["High-dose systemic Prednisone", "Combined Dabrafenib and Trametinib", "Intravenous Cisplatin and 5-Fluorouracil", "Vismodegib (or Sonidegib)", "Pembrolizumab"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For metastatic or locally advanced BCC that is not amenable to surgery or radiation, the Hedgehog pathway inhibitors Vismodegib or Sonidegib are indicated first-line. These are small-molecule inhibitors of Smoothened (SMO), which block the dysregulated, overactive signaling pathway characteristic of BCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16212: A patient with advanced Basal Cell Carcinoma is treated with the Smoothened (SMO) inhibitor Vismodegib. Three months into therapy, they present with severe muscle cramps, hair loss (alopecia), and complete loss of taste (ageusia). What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Initiate high-potency oral calcium channel blockers for muscle cramps", "Recognize these as classic, treatment-limiting side effects of Hedgehog inhibitors and consider a drug holiday or dose adjustment", "Add systemic glucocorticoids to counteract the taste loss and alopecia", "Immediately screen the patient for acute motor neuropathy", "Discontinue the drug permanently as these side effects indicate life-threatening toxicity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hedgehog inhibitors (Vismodegib, Sonidegib) are notoriously associated with muscle spasms/cramps, alopecia, and dysgeusia/ageusia (taste loss). These are class-specific, treatment-limiting side effects that frequently lead to poor patient compliance and necessitate drug holidays, dosage reductions, or supportive care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61649: A 72-year-old girl with a history of significant sun exposure presents with multiple rough, sand-paper-like red spots on their bald scalp and ears. A biopsy of one lesion shows partial-thickness epidermal dysplasia with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ)", "Discoidal Lupus Erythematosus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Actinic Keratosis", "Seborrheic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Actinic Keratosis (AK) is a very common intraepithelial precursor to invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC). It presents clinically as rough, gritty, sandpaper-like scaly papules on chronically sun-exposed skin. Histopathology classically shows partial-thickness atypical keratinocytes in the basal/spinous layers and the ''flag sign'' (alternating columns of orthokeratosis and parakeratosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #44341: A dermatologist evaluates a 78-year-old male with hundreds of actinic keratoses on their face, forearms, and scalp. Rather than freeze each lesion individually with liquid nitrogen, the clinician prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil 5% cream to be applied to the entire face. What clinical concept justifies this field-directed treatment?', '["Keratolytic occlusion", "Metastatic suppression", "Contact hypersensitivity induction", "Field cancerization", "Autoimmune desensitization"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The concept of ''field cancerization'' states that chronically sun-damaged skin contains widespread, subclinical genetic alterations and microscopic dysplastic clones, putting the entire area at high risk for multiple future skin cancers. Field therapies (such as topical 5-FU, imiquimod, or photodynamic therapy) target both visible lesions and subclinical dysplasia across the entire field.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69523: A 12-year-old male presents with a slow-growing, well-demarcated, 2 cm, red, scaly plaque on their lower leg. Biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal dysplasia with highly atypical keratinocytes, mitotic figures, and multinucleated giant cells, with an intact basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Keratoacanthoma", "Bowen''s disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ)", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Superficial Basal Cell Carcinoma", "Actinic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bowen''s disease is the clinical term for Squamous Cell Carcinoma in-situ (SCCIS). Histologically, it is characterized by full-thickness dysplasia of the epidermis (disorganized, ''windblown'' appearance, cellular atypia, mitotic figures) but with a completely intact basement membrane (no dermal invasion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63049: A 52-year-old uncircumcised male presents with a painless, bright red, velvety plaque on the glans penis that has been slowly expanding over 1 year. Biopsy reveals full-thickness squamous dysplasia confined to the epithelium. What is the specific clinical term for this presentation?', '["Pearly penile papules", "Erythroplasia of Queyrat", "Bowenoid papulosis", "Balanitis xerotica obliterans", "Condyloma acuminata"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythroplasia of Queyrat is the specific clinical term for Bowen''s disease (SCC in-situ) arising on the mucosal or transitional surfaces of the glans penis or prepuce in uncircumcised males. It classically presents as a bright red, velvety, slightly elevated plaque.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98840: A 55-year-old girl presents with a rapidly growing, painful, firm, hyperkeratotic nodule with a central ulcer on the vermilion border of their lower lip. They have a history of smoking and outdoor work. What is the most critical prognostic risk factor associated with this lesion compared to a similar lesion on the cheek?', '["It is driven by HPV and is infectious", "The anatomical location (lower lip) carries a significantly higher risk of regional lymph node metastasis", "It has a high rate of spontaneous regression", "It is highly likely to be driven by a PTCH1 mutation", "It is highly resistant to surgical excision"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising on high-risk sites like the lower lip (vermilion border) or the ear has a significantly higher rate of regional lymph node metastasis (approx 10-15%) compared to typical SCCs arising within actinic keratoses on chronically sun-damaged hair-bearing facial skin (approx 1%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78476: A 25-year-old man organ transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression (cyclosporine, prednisone, and mycophenolate mofetil) presents for a routine skin check. What is the relative risk and clinical behavior of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) in this patient population?', '["An increased risk of melanoma, but a decreased risk of non-melanoma skin cancer", "A slightly decreased risk of SCC due to the anti-inflammatory effects of prednisone", "A 65- to 100-fold increased risk of developing SCC, with a higher risk of multiple, aggressive tumors and metastasis", "The risk is identical to the general population", "A high risk of benign viral warts, but no increase in malignant transformation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Organ transplant recipients on long-term systemic immunosuppressants have a dramatically elevated risk of skin cancer, most notably a 65- to 100-fold increased risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC), which frequently presents as multiple, rapidly growing, highly aggressive tumors with a high rate of recurrence and metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37678: A 68-year-old girl presents with a symmetric, dome-shaped, 2 cm skin nodule on their forearm that appeared and grew to its current size in just 4 weeks. The nodule features a prominent, crater-like central keratinous plug. What is the characteristic biological behavior of this lesion?', '["Indolent growth over decades with no metastatic potential", "Rapid initial growth followed by a period of stability and possible spontaneous involution over several months, though it is clinically managed as a well-differentiated SCC", "Continuous, aggressive growth with high rates of early distant metastasis to the lungs", "Development of multiple satellite lesions in a sporotrichoid pattern", "Infiltration into the deep fascia and bone within weeks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical description of a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (''volcano-like'') that grows rapidly over a few weeks is classic for a Keratoacanthoma (KA). A characteristic feature of KAs is their potential for spontaneous regression (involution). However, because they can be histologically and clinically indistinguishable from invasive, well-differentiated SCC, they are treated surgically as SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18238: A 58-year-old woman presents with a non-healing, enlarging, and painful ulcer within a deep scar on their lower leg, where they sustained a severe third-degree burn 25 years ago. Biopsy of the everted edge of the ulcer confirms invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Cushing''s ulcer", "Venous stasis ulcer", "Marjolin''s ulcer", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Curling''s ulcer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Marjolin''s ulcer is an aggressive Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) arising within an area of chronic inflammation, trauma, or scarring—classically old burn scars, osteomyelitis sinus tracts, or chronic venous ulcers. These tumors have a significantly higher rate of metastasis (up to 30%) than typical cutaneous SCCs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18008: A 12-year-old female presents with a mole on their back that has become darker and larger over the past year. Physical exam shows a 9mm asymmetric plaque with irregular borders, color variegation (brown, black, and red), and a small area of central gray-white scaling. What is the most common subtype of melanoma overall in fair-skinned populations?', '["Nodular Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Superficial Spreading Melanoma (SSM) is the most common subtype of melanoma in Caucasians, accounting for approximately 70% of all cases. It classically presents with the classic ABCDE warning signs, initially undergoing a prolonged radial growth phase (spreading horizontally in the epidermis) before entering the vertical growth phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93885: A 20-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing, dark blue-black, dome-shaped nodule on their arm that appeared 2 months ago. Unlike their other flat moles, this lesion is uniform in color and symmetric. Biopsy reveals a malignant melanocytic tumor that lacks a significant radial growth phase. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Desmoplastic Melanoma", "Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nodular Melanoma is the second most common subtype and is highly aggressive. It characteristically lacks a significant radial growth phase, entering the vertical growth phase (growing downward into the dermis) almost immediately. Because of this, it often presents as a symmetric, dome-shaped nodule that fails several classic ''ABCDE'' criteria (e.g., it can be symmetric and uniform), but has a prominent ''E'' (Evolving).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75975: An 82-year-old woman presents with a large, flat, brown, ''smudge-like'' patch on their cheek that has been slowly expanding over the past 10 years. Physical exam reveals a 3.5 cm ill-defined macule with irregular brown-black pigmentation on a background of severe wrinkling and solar elastosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Melasma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Seborrheic Keratosis", "Lentigo Maligna (Melanoma in-situ)", "Actinic Keratosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lentigo Maligna (LM) is melanoma in-situ arising on chronically sun-damaged skin, most commonly the face or neck of elderly individuals. It classically presents as a slowly expanding, flat, variegated brown-to-black macule with very poorly defined clinical borders. Once it invades the dermis, it is termed Lentigo Maligna Melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39139: A 65-year-old African American man presents with a dark, irregular patch on the sole of their foot. They note it has been present for 2 years and they originally thought it was a bruise from walking. Physical exam shows a 2.5 cm asymmetric, variegated black and gray macule on the right heel. What is the subtype of this melanoma?', '["Lentigo Maligna Melanoma", "Nodular Melanoma", "Superficial Spreading Melanoma", "Acral Lentiginous Melanoma", "Mucosal Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM) arises on the palms, soles, or nail apparatus (subungual). While rare overall, it is the most common subtype of melanoma in non-Caucasian populations (Black, Asian, Hispanic). Notably, ALM is NOT related to UV light/sun exposure, which explains its occurrence on non-sun-exposed acral sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37433: A 72-year-old woman presents with a dark brown longitudinal band on their right thumbnail. The band is 4mm wide, irregular, and has become wider over 1 year. The clinician notes brown pigmentation extending onto the proximal nail fold (eponychium). What is this clinical sign called, and what does it suggest?', '["Nikolsky''s sign, suggesting pemphigus vulgaris", "Schwartz sign, indicative of otosclerosis", "Auspitz sign, indicative of nail psoriasis", "Hutchinson''s sign, highly suggestive of subungual melanoma", "Darier''s sign, suggesting mastocytosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hutchinson''s sign in dermatology refers to the extension of pigment from the nail matrix/plate onto the proximal or lateral nail fold (the periungual skin). It is a highly sensitive clinical indicator of subungual melanoma (which is a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11237: A 35-year-old woman presents with a pink, rapidly growing, glistening nodule on their shoulder that bleeds easily. The lesion has no brown or black pigment and resembles a pyogenic granuloma. Biopsy of the lesion reveals atypical melanocytes that stain positive for S100 and SOX10. What is this clinical variant called?', '["Pyogenic granuloma", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Amelanotic Melanoma", "Spitz nevus", "Nodular Basal Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amelanotic Melanoma is a classic ''diagnostic trap'' because it lacks visible melanin pigment. It presents as a pink, red, or flesh-colored papule or nodule, often mimicking benign vascular lesions (like pyogenic granuloma) or non-melanoma skin cancers. Pathologists use immunohistochemical stains (S100, SOX10, Melan-A) to confirm the melanocytic lineage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11422: A 12-year-old woman undergoes a narrow-margin excisional biopsy for a suspicious pigmented lesion on their back. The pathology report confirms melanoma. Which of the following histological parameters is the single most important prognostic factor for primary cutaneous melanoma?', '["Clark''s level of anatomic invasion", "Breslow depth (measured in millimeters)", "The presence of regression", "The presence of a lymphocytic infiltrate", "The subtype of melanoma (e.g., SSM vs. ALM)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Breslow depth—the vertical measurement in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis to the deepest malignant melanocyte in the dermis—is the single most important prognostic factor for primary, localized cutaneous melanoma, and directly determines the T-category and surgical margin requirements.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87692: A pathology report for a primary cutaneous melanoma excision states: ''Breslow depth 1.2 mm, ulceration absent, mitoses 2/mm2.'' The surgical margins are clear. What is the next standard step in the clinical management of this patient?', '["CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with IV contrast only", "Wide local excision with 1-2 cm margins and consideration of a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB)", "No further surgery; follow up with serial clinical checks every 6 months", "Prophylactic complete lymph node dissection of the regional basin", "Immediate initiation of systemic adjuvant pembrolizumab therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For intermediate-thickness melanomas (Breslow depth 1.0 - 4.0 mm, or >0.8 mm with high-risk features like ulceration or high mitoses), the standard of care is a wide local excision (typically 1-2 cm margins) combined with a Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) to evaluate the regional lymph node basin for micro-metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #99852: Genetic testing of a metastatic melanoma specimen from a 68-year-old man reveals a mutation in the BRAF gene at codon 600, resulting in the substitution of valine for glutamic acid (V600E). What is the normal physiological function of the BRAF protein?', '["A lysosomal enzyme involved in antigen processing", "A serine/threonine kinase that is a component of the MAP kinase signaling pathway", "A cell surface receptor for Epidermal Growth Factor (EGFR)", "A cell-cycle checkpoint protein that acts as a G1/S gatekeeper", "A nuclear transcription factor regulating tumor suppressor proteins"]'::jsonb, 1, 'BRAF is a proto-oncogene encoding a serine/threonine protein kinase that is a key component of the intracellular MAP kinase (RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK) signaling pathway. The V600E mutation leads to constitutive, ligand-independent activation of the pathway, driving cell proliferation and survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88157: A patient with BRAF V600E mutated metastatic melanoma is initiated on treatment with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., Dabrafenib) combined with a MEK inhibitor (e.g., Trametinib). What is the primary clinical benefit of combining these two targeted agents rather than using a BRAF inhibitor alone?', '["It prevents the development of systemic hypertension", "It avoids the need for periodic liver function test monitoring", "It allows the patient to continue therapy without any risk of QT prolongation", "It eliminates the risk of drug-induced hyperthermia and fever", "It delays the onset of resistance and decreases the rate of paradoxical pathway activation that causes secondary Squamous Cell Carcinomas"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Monotherapy with a BRAF inhibitor (e.g., vemurafenib, dabrafenib) leads to rapid resistance via alternate pathway activation. Furthermore, it causes paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do not have the BRAF V600E mutation, leading to the development of secondary cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinomas and Keratoacanthomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor blocks this paradoxical signaling, reducing secondary skin cancers and improving progression-free survival.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34621: A 17-year-old boy with Stage IV melanoma is initiated on Pembrolizumab. What is the direct molecular mechanism of action of this medication?', '["Recombinant interleukin that directly stimulates cytotoxic T-cell proliferation", "Humanized monoclonal antibody that blocks the Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-lymphocytes, preventing tumor-mediated immune evasion", "Chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to CTLA-4 to deplete regulatory T-cells", "Monoclonal antibody targeting the CD20 antigen on circulating B-cells", "Small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor (VEGFR)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells. Tumor cells often express PD-L1 (Programmed Death Ligand 1) to bind PD-1, sending an inhibitory signal that turns off the T-cell (immune evasion). Blocking this interaction ''releases the brakes'' on the immune system, allowing tumor-specific T-cells to attack the cancer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #14326: A patient with advanced melanoma is treated with a combination of Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) and Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4). Two months into therapy, they present with severe watery diarrhea (6 bowel movements per day), abdominal cramping, and a high fever. What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?', '["Direct secretory drug toxicity; discontinue the immunotherapy and initiate loperamide", "Irritable bowel syndrome flare; initiate dietary modifications", "Immune-related colitis; treat with systemic high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone/prednisone)", "Infectious salmonella gastroenteritis; treat with oral Ciprofloxacin", "Clostridioides difficile colitis; treat with oral Vancomycin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitors (especially combination anti-PD-1/anti-CTLA-4) are notorious for causing immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs) due to autoimmune activation. Colitis is a classic, severe irAE presenting with diarrhea and abdominal pain. Once infectious causes are ruled out, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe irAEs is high-dose systemic corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #51479: A 25-year-old female presents with a history of over 100 atypical-appearing melanocytic nevi across their trunk and limbs. Their father and paternal grandfather were both diagnosed with cutaneous melanoma before age 40. Genetic analysis reveals a germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene. What is this hereditary cancer syndrome, and what other internal malignancy is this patient at high risk for?', '["Gorlin syndrome; increased risk of Medulloblastoma", "Muir-Torre syndrome; increased risk of Colorectal Carcinoma", "Li-Fraumeni syndrome; increased risk of Osteosarcoma and breast cancer", "Cowden syndrome; increased risk of Thyroid Follicular Carcinoma", "Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome; increased risk of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Familial Atypical Multiple Mole-Melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple atypical nevi, a strong family history of melanoma, and an associated germline mutation in the CDKN2A gene (encoding the p16 tumor suppressor). FAMMM syndrome carries a highly elevated risk of developing both cutaneous melanoma and pancreatic adenocarcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #52913: A pathologist reviews an excisional biopsy of a melanoma from a 25-year-old boy. A large portion of the central tumor shows a dense infiltrate of CD8+ T-lymphocytes, dermal fibrosis, melanophages, and a complete absence of melanoma cells. What is this phenomenon, and what challenge does it present?', '["Tumor regression; extensive regression (>75%) makes the true maximum Breslow depth difficult to determine as the thickest part may have been immunologically destroyed", "Tumor ulceration; it indicates rapid tumor necrosis and carries a highly unfavorable prognosis", "Desmoplasia; it makes the tumor highly resistant to systemic checkpoint immunotherapy", "Pagetoid scatter; it indicates the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase", "Koebner response; it represents mechanical trauma leading to tumor spread"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Melanoma regression is an immune-mediated host response where CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocytes attack and destroy tumor cells, leaving behind a scarred, vascular, depigmented zone. While it represents an active immune response, extensive regression (>75% of the lesion) is problematic because the thickest, most prognostic portion of the original tumor may have been destroyed, resulting in an under-measurement of the Breslow depth.');
